eratorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649468} {:answer/id #uuid "85567da8-12fb-51a7-a89b-c7aa7d63402c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649509} {:answer/id #uuid "c618b929-05f9-57b7-b556-abad3e8a3db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649618} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc22a7c-ec0a-514b-9331-ab5ef46ce421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-1}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdc22a7c-ec0a-514b-9331-ab5ef46ce421" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-1}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649631} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b842398-5d6d-55b6-b884-40f4320f9c66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "077be596-5e1b-5388-8714-3b1435ad3d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-93909" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+3t{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+3t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+3\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda +1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =-1.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-1}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649827 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6b2a08-3631-58ca-b84d-90640f3275cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{5}+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649340} {:answer/id #uuid "3accbfb2-9367-54c9-bf66-8707d8f31701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649365} {:answer/id #uuid "3e285849-91d0-599d-b8f2-c4b77bd1e2a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}+{c}_{2}{x}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649375} {:answer/id #uuid "65447a66-38b4-531a-819b-811e352c3a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{5}+{c}_{2}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649456} {:answer/id #uuid "c6b89296-6b8a-5b9a-9e3d-50f3a5f8402a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65447a66-38b4-531a-819b-811e352c3a5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{5}+{c}_{2}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649456} :answer-field/id #uuid "949d2f95-7909-552c-9df2-d3dd127f4266" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3482da75-499a-5b2b-ad3b-551b6c1166ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-102347" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-9x{y}^{′}+25y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-9x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-9\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +25] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =5.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{5}+{c}_{2}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649838 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086e4748-56b4-5b91-84e3-1360ad6c7c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649282} {:answer/id #uuid "30a3a63f-a430-5204-a181-333151b01e29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649347} {:answer/id #uuid "8e3efa0c-294e-5419-840e-4d8a0b5cc98e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649522} {:answer/id #uuid "a22dfc2f-17f7-506a-97a2-f23c57f1791e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649565} {:answer/id #uuid "cf73df73-8e46-55e4-86ba-9956b3195ceb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "086e4748-56b4-5b91-84e3-1360ad6c7c1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649282} :answer-field/id #uuid "947c031b-6988-5282-96dc-dc50cdd4a3bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "47ff09d9-9cdf-50ea-a49c-a926fff0ff52" :question/math-academy-id "q-93908" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+9y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-5x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+9{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-5\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+9{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-5\\lambda +9] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-5\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =3.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186661528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1490f580-a5fd-5309-9a95-5bd0e746f593" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{8}+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661008} {:answer/id #uuid "83835ac1-0194-53a5-b7e9-025dbf77ad41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{8}+{c}_{2}{x}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661198} {:answer/id #uuid "b741a825-342d-5957-8cf6-12c9a9e1f597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{8}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661290} {:answer/id #uuid "d311396c-5b4f-51eb-8e93-93e6770187f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661330} {:answer/id #uuid "f05d27c0-548b-5f07-891a-adcbf3d8493d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{4}+{c}_{2}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83835ac1-0194-53a5-b7e9-025dbf77ad41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{8}+{c}_{2}{x}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661198} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9ed4e7a-3877-516d-a697-25de549a5489" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7cf425f9-68be-5119-bc9e-7c7d407dbf13" :question/math-academy-id "q-64879" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-15x{y}^{′}+64y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-15x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+64{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-15\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+64{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-15\\lambda +64] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-15\\lambda +64 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-16\\lambda +64 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -8)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =8.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{8}+{c}_{2}{x}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with a Repeated Root"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147354 :question/id #uuid "ff0b0603-e0bf-526a-9b4b-d2a4886ba2c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-6628" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' - 19 xy' + 100y = 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny =c_1 x^{10}+ c_2x ^{10}\\ln x ,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' - 19 xy' + 100y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0,\\quad  y'(1)=1, \\quad x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0= c_1.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 1.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =10{c}_{1}{x}^{9}+{c}_{2}{x}^{9}+10{c}_{2}{x}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(1)=1$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =10{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 1 & =0+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = x ^{10}\\ln x .\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f9ca4b7-157d-56c9-9302-e7ad08c615c0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d1d0839-8119-503d-93fc-b968d7cbba14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\frac{x^{2} + 5}{2x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146871} {:answer/id #uuid "d015b352-f306-5030-9862-94e6cc42c090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = x^{3}(3 - 17\\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146872} {:answer/id #uuid "e2c4cb4e-32f3-59ca-a00a-6a1de55ce41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = x(3 - \\ln x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146873} {:answer/id #uuid "384f6e8c-dfe4-58b5-b065-0ef25b92bb50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\frac{17 + x^{6}}{6x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146874} {:answer/id #uuid "4b993516-1b2d-55f8-b823-07beaea047c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\frac{3 + \\ln x}{x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b993516-1b2d-55f8-b823-07beaea047c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x) = \\frac{3 + \\ln x}{x^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146875} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eb6aa3b-e9a1-5b8c-82f1-af08bd0fa3df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146870}] :question/id #uuid "8c5f77fe-b09a-56b3-a0f6-bf761de278c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-64778" :question/problem "Find the solution of the initial value problem\n$$\n\\displaystyle x^{2}y^{″} + 7xy^{′} + 9y = 0, y(1) = 3, y^{′}(1) =-8, x > 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The characteristic equation is\n$$\n\\lambda(\\lambda-1)+7\\lambda+9=\\lambda^2+6\\lambda+9=(\\lambda+3)^2=0.\n$$\nThe repeated root $-3$ gives\n$$\ny=c_1x^{-3}+c_2x^{-3}\\ln x.\n$$\nThe condition $y(1)=3$ gives $c_1=3.$ Differentiating,\n$$\ny'=-3c_1x^{-4}+c_2x^{-4}-3c_2x^{-4}\\ln x.\n$$\nUsing $y'(1)=-8$ gives $-3c_1+c_2=-8,$ so $c_2=1.$ Therefore, for $x>0,$\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{3+\\ln x}{x^3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148191 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b533a07a-2e0b-5a93-b8b4-8ea517acc9de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = 3t^{2} - t^{2}\\ln t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146901} {:answer/id #uuid "16ba5716-e0f1-507a-b29a-a99c7e8739ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = t^{2} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146902} {:answer/id #uuid "2e289c91-8c17-5d54-8e3c-9c9df08d44d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = 3t^{2}\\ln t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146903} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8f00ed-26ad-51b2-9b0b-b02effa6e859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = 2t^{2} + t^{2}\\ln t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146904} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca6d5e2-21cf-5106-a3a8-337ad489c255" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = 3\\ln t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e289c91-8c17-5d54-8e3c-9c9df08d44d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t) = 3t^{2}\\ln t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146903} :answer-field/id #uuid "0355cf4f-1541-5c9f-ade1-067716169606" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186146900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb282e4d-658f-5955-aa68-61cd7cfcadc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-64881" :question/problem "Find the solution of the initial value problem\n$$\n\\displaystyle 2t^{2}y^{″} - 6ty^{′} + 8y = 0, y(1) = 0, y^{′}(1) = 3, t > 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-6t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-6\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+8{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-6\\lambda +8] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ 2({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4) & =0 \\\\ 2(\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =2.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln} t.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=3.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2t{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}t+2{c}_{2}t\\operatorname{ln} t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ and ${c}_{1}=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =2{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 3 & =0+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=3{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln} t.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce6005b-274a-5b52-abf7-0cb69b215909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{4+14\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235885} {:answer/id #uuid "2a25c4ce-ed4c-5666-ad4c-e53a2e857b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{4-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236079} {:answer/id #uuid "81eab79e-fcf6-5feb-aa5e-16cb0bfb4423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2+14\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236626} {:answer/id #uuid "8e491e78-c75e-57e3-a10d-5fd070664021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2-14\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236718} {:answer/id #uuid "cd598184-c61c-5292-ac3a-23ba58cce7b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{4-16\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ce6005b-274a-5b52-abf7-0cb69b215909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{4+14\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235885} :answer-field/id #uuid "df9d011a-5996-5e24-8649-b212b7ef48b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a8ac38ea-1f77-5f1f-95a7-c89e02bd2ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-102364" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+13x{y}^{′}+36y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{-6}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+13x{y}^{′}+36y=0,\\,y(1)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=-10,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=4.$ Substituting $y(1)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n4={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=-10.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-6{c}_{1}{x}^{-7}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-7}-6{c}_{2}{x}^{-7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=-10$ and ${c}_{1}=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-10 & =-6(4)+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =4{x}^{-6}+14{x}^{-6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{4+14\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{6}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05bf703b-213d-5853-963f-5bd3b0167d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2x-5x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354702} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed22a9e-d85f-5a1e-9356-d2763ae75f59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{x}{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355646} {:answer/id #uuid "90a4e36c-4a86-5551-a5a1-0d04491243dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=x-4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355940} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5bb300-699d-5967-b650-dc9d7bdc1238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356429} {:answer/id #uuid "d91209b9-7435-577c-9930-c1a0600d9833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=x+3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d91209b9-7435-577c-9930-c1a0600d9833" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=x+3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356570} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5bbe535-449b-5b39-b27a-6d5a07c96c23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9340f8a4-c84d-5e82-96e6-14527bafdc56" :question/math-academy-id "q-93910" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=4.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =1+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x+3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d367df6-38a5-545c-9534-618370b9b9cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-2{x}^{4}+3{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354787} {:answer/id #uuid "21452eae-ba70-52ce-bf30-3b644ce9c44d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{4}-2{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354945} {:answer/id #uuid "260009a9-381f-5751-a6ce-3a0fcf933f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{4}-3{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354984} {:answer/id #uuid "3d619ef8-599b-5e25-b301-23c1e986f16b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{4}-{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355212} {:answer/id #uuid "7d588824-ac66-54e3-ba55-9b0fa8accc73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=-3{x}^{4}-4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21452eae-ba70-52ce-bf30-3b644ce9c44d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{4}-2{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354945} :answer-field/id #uuid "d363665a-4fcd-52af-84a9-12c70a3b3b17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ef38efc5-da6e-573f-9791-529409cb51bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-93912" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-7x{y}^{′}+16y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{4}+{c}_{2}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-7x{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=6,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=2.$ Substituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=6.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =4{c}_{1}{x}^{3}+{c}_{2}{x}^{3}+4{c}_{2}{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=6$ and ${c}_{1}=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =4(2)+{c}_{2} \\\\ 6 & =8+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=2{x}^{4}-2{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "078a8ab0-d566-53a1-85d4-293646597824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2+13\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354729} {:answer/id #uuid "21747374-92b5-568e-95ac-6e8a93be37ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{3-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354947} {:answer/id #uuid "87f2f522-9e57-5ad2-a4c5-bcba3218d4a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355867} {:answer/id #uuid "aa159bee-756f-50fa-8e3e-ce1a881c2497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{4-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356169} {:answer/id #uuid "c64fd3c6-584d-54be-91bd-30cf3c2aecf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "078a8ab0-d566-53a1-85d4-293646597824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2+13\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354729} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f29098-0c56-58f8-a2cc-a7cdbdacacc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "42c24349-6cdd-5b59-9ed6-ba198c334780" :question/math-academy-id "q-93914" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+11x{y}^{′}+25y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+11x{y}^{′}+25y=0,\\,y(1)=2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=2.$ Substituting $y(1)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=3.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-5{c}_{1}{x}^{-6}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-6}-5{c}_{2}{x}^{-6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ and ${c}_{1}=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =-5(2)+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =2{x}^{-5}+13{x}^{-5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{2+13\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26503b29-5d97-5698-9856-731c91189fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=3{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354987} {:answer/id #uuid "9ecd89cb-8c63-5ac6-b87d-ef24046664ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2{t}^{9}+3{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356078} {:answer/id #uuid "aaaaa9a6-e18f-5b43-b062-909befb9e899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356174} {:answer/id #uuid "ae962cc9-644f-5bd8-ba08-86df2433cb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=4{t}^{9}-3{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356202} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a2d3a4-dd04-59ac-bc32-b5db7f6b6faf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=-2{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356689}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26503b29-5d97-5698-9856-731c91189fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=3{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354987} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d43ad62-447f-5154-9458-2b8539dd8b1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bd788041-dc66-5514-9b2b-394d5d0e328a" :question/math-academy-id "q-93915" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-17t{y}^{′}+81y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{t}^{9}+{c}_{2}{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-17t{y}^{′}+81y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=3.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =9{c}_{1}{t}^{8}+{c}_{2}{t}^{8}+9{c}_{2}{t}^{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ and ${c}_{1}=0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =9{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 3 & =0+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =3{t}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386007 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "380571d6-ad52-5c32-9ec7-dfff8cf3080b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{1-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386009} {:answer/id #uuid "e21e0f1d-fc76-5b8e-802f-cf0520147a52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2-8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386010} {:answer/id #uuid "e2253b63-3223-5268-8ccd-867c548e6701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{2-12\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386011} {:answer/id #uuid "22fc0b9a-6729-55c7-8806-3db09893929a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{1+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386012} {:answer/id #uuid "7870f20c-2346-588a-b19f-7f5eb3e72c57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{1+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22fc0b9a-6729-55c7-8806-3db09893929a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=\\frac{1+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{7}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386012} :answer-field/id #uuid "46f0be38-8313-521b-b53a-affa294b3539" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b2f1e33b-97ff-5879-aa46-ccc76aa7159d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102365" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+15x{y}^{′}+49y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{-7}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+15x{y}^{′}+49y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-7{c}_{1}{x}^{-8}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-8}-7{c}_{2}{x}^{-8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-7(1)+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={x}^{-7}+8{x}^{-7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bee072d2-4577-556e-9c87-f8cd21946112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392907} {:answer/id #uuid "3b98e7ee-f4b5-55c7-b5b3-42a2da764140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{-5}(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392908} {:answer/id #uuid "17d0323e-a430-57f7-8685-c0bb5e6b3131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)={x}^{-5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392909} {:answer/id #uuid "7ec65102-49b4-5387-b6bb-bedd5d1879fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{5}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392910} {:answer/id #uuid "ccde75e1-f7c8-5c92-a38b-d5665342b07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{5}(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccde75e1-f7c8-5c92-a38b-d5665342b07e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(x)=2{x}^{5}(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392911} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d62fa2a-d4d4-5b32-9ed7-a4abdfec29be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392906}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5be694f6-babf-5a30-89e1-81d884ac107b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64883" :question/problem "Find the solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-9x{y}^{′}+25y=0,\\,y(1)=-2,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-9x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-9\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+25{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +25] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-9\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =5.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{5}+{c}_{2}{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=-2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n-2={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=4.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =5{x}^{4}{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{4}+5{c}_{2}{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ and ${c}_{1}=-2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =5{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 4 & =-10+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =-2{x}^{5}+14{x}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =2{x}^{5}(7\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392912 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c367b6e-fea7-510f-99b8-b52133b254fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{6}(4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392914} {:answer/id #uuid "20f374aa-7dba-5a73-8278-84cb5a4bba14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=4{t}^{6}(2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392915} {:answer/id #uuid "f60e006b-f392-517f-8973-8ebb3b0c527f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=4{t}^{6}(1-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392916} {:answer/id #uuid "271b1a20-b2e0-5a35-8330-17a74aa8cce1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2{t}^{-6}(1-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392917} {:answer/id #uuid "77a91d39-e494-559a-b713-8c49400438b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{-6}(1+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392918}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f60e006b-f392-517f-8973-8ebb3b0c527f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=4{t}^{6}(1-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392916} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ec9bf5-210b-5464-8c5b-59d19bf3af3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "92580c12-a8bb-5712-ad7a-3fb0808253a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-64886" :question/problem "Find the solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-11t{y}^{′}+36y=0,\\,y(1)=4,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=0,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}-11t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+36{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }-11\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+36{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-11\\lambda +36] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-11\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =6.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{6}+{c}_{2}{t}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n4={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=0.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =6{c}_{1}{t}^{5}+{c}_{2}{t}^{5}+6{c}_{2}{t}^{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=0$ and ${c}_{1}=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =6{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 0 & =24+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =4{t}^{6}-24{t}^{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & =4{t}^{6}(1-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186649848 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a834485-f79b-5f15-bdce-7165deae935a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{3t-9\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649513} {:answer/id #uuid "8eee944d-4694-5a58-af06-09fd84f98f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{3+15\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649525} {:answer/id #uuid "a9040967-da70-51ab-ab3b-5d9a1e768c64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{4}(3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649574} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c7764e-adaa-578f-9df1-df826b8efdfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{3+9\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649699} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbfb18c-cad4-5279-9265-2fd252fb7d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{4}(15+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eee944d-4694-5a58-af06-09fd84f98f32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=\\frac{3+15\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649525} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd568edd-d2f7-58c4-a520-a1d65a66b00a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186649791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5d4dd6b2-e55e-56ba-ba04-ff57addd7ee7" :question/math-academy-id "q-64884" :question/problem "Find the solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}+9t{y}^{′}+16y=0,\\,y(1)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=3,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+9t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}+16{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+9\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }+16{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+9\\lambda +16] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+9\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =-4.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-4}+{c}_{2}{t}^{-4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{t}^{4}}+\\frac{{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n3={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=3.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{4{c}_{1}}{{t}^{5}}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{t}^{5}}-\\frac{4{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)}{{t}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=3$ and ${c}_{1}=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =-4{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 3 & =-12+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(t)=\\frac{3+15\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}{{t}^{4}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186661539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064ec850-766c-55c5-b7ee-979469b544af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186660983} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba9ebbe-634e-575a-80ab-de003c475749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661081} {:answer/id #uuid "4868331e-0c52-5e43-b831-3863870857ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661109} {:answer/id #uuid "da123301-e626-5f15-b61b-2d8febd9c81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661338} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8e08bd-e78f-5adb-88cb-b87f887a13e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661343}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc8e08bd-e78f-5adb-88cb-b87f887a13e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{x}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661343} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c490ab-96e2-5bec-a318-ccc247ee5e89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9413b2c3-6708-59b3-bbda-94cea09d60fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-64880" :question/problem "Find the solution of the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+3x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(2)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1,\\,x>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+3x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+3\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda +1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+3\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =-1.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{-1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}+\\frac{{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(2)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+\\frac{{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{1}=-{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(2)=1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(2)=1$ and ${c}_{1}=-{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{4}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{4}-\\frac{{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{4} \\\\ 4 & =-{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}-{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 4 & ={c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{c}_{2}-{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 4 & ={c}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}=-{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & =-\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{x}+\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x}\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186661558 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "293f6891-aa69-594f-bf87-564974a35c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=-2{t}^{3}-{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661049} {:answer/id #uuid "52367655-0ef4-5d84-8052-28af07529124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{3}+2{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661131} {:answer/id #uuid "8b0a1f43-f71c-5fda-bff2-1d5d523bc1f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)=2{t}^{3}+{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661209} {:answer/id #uuid "dc996164-b860-5b00-bff0-e6f62875442d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{3}-{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661344} {:answer/id #uuid "e0bb4085-1529-5b62-abbf-4a9942a15144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{3}-2{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52367655-0ef4-5d84-8052-28af07529124" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y(t)={t}^{3}+2{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661131} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a181430-fc3a-5361-addc-36fb7286231e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186661443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d0489779-1a28-591d-bc25-3a35b66c5824" :question/math-academy-id "q-93913" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-5t{y}^{′}+9y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{t}^{3}+{c}_{2}{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}{y}^{″}-5t{y}^{′}+9y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=5,\\,t>0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=5.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{c}_{1}{t}^{2}+{c}_{2}{t}^{2}+3{c}_{2}{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=5$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =3{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 5 & =3+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & ={t}^{3}+2{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem Involving a Cauchy-Euler Equation with a Repeated Root"}] :topic/math-academy-id 2544 :topic/title "Cauchy-Euler Equations: Characteristic Equations With Repeated Roots"}]}
